## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates dynamic programming skills with state expansion, requiring candidates to track whether the cell removal has been used. It tests proper handling of grid boundary conditions and provides opportunities to discuss space optimization strategies and compare memoization versus tabulation approaches.

## Key Concepts
- DP with auxiliary state tracking
- Space optimization using rolling arrays
- Boundary condition handling
- State transition logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPathWithOneRemoval(grid) {
  const m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
  const dp0 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(Infinity));
  const dp1 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(Infinity));

  dp0[0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp1[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (i > 0) {
        dp0[i][j] = Math.min(dp0[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp0[i-1][j]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp1[i-1][j]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], dp0[i-1][j]);
      }
      if (j > 0) {
        dp0[i][j] = Math.min(dp0[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp0[i][j-1]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], grid[i][j] + dp1[i][j-1]);
        dp1[i][j] = Math.min(dp1[i][j], dp0[i][j-1]);
      }
    }
  }

  return Math.min(dp0[m-1][n-1], dp1[m-1][n-1]);
}
```

## Time/Space Complexity
- Time: O(m × n) - we process each cell once
- Space: O(m × n) - can be optimized to O(n) using rolling arrays since we only need the previous row

## Why This Is Asked
Path auto-complete is a common editor feature; it probes practical data-structure trade-offs beyond textbooks.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node children map
- Prefix search via traversal then DFS for completions
- Memory sharing of prefixes and separator handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.isEnd = false;
  }
}
class PathTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insertPath(path) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of path) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.isEnd = true;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, maxResults) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    const dfs = (cur, built) => {
      if (results.length >= maxResults) return;
      if (cur.isEnd) results.push(prefix + built);
      for (const [ch, child] of cur.next) dfs(child, built + ch);
    };
    dfs(node, "");
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for memory with extremely large path samples (e.g., thousands of common prefixes)?
- How would you support ranking suggestions by frequency or recency without breaking O(length) per insert?

Trie advantages: inserts/finds traverse a path matching the word, sharing prefixes reduces duplication; autocomplete across a prefix becomes a subtree traversal, yielding all completions efficiently. Hash maps store exact keys; prefix queries require scanning all keys or maintaining inverted indexes, which is costly. Trade-offs: Tries incur higher pointer/structural overhead per node and can be memory-heavy with large alphabets or sparse usage; compression (Patricia/radix) or fixed-size arrays can mitigate this. Deletion is non-trivial due to shared prefixes. Real-world nuance: store frequencies at nodes to surface top-k suggestions, or prune with a capped DFS for responsive UI.

